WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.399
Carter Thallon: On this episode of the

00:00:01.399 --> 00:00:04.340
"Pop Culture Decoded" show,
it's all "Breaking Bad."

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.410
We look at how "Breaking
Bad" relates to Westerns

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.090
and "The Godfather," we
dissect an iconic scene,

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:11.420
and we'll break down a new teaser

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.236
for the "El Camino" movie.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.917
With the release of the
upcoming "Breaking Bad" movie,

00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:25.100
"El Camino," we wanted to take a look back

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.030
at the original series
and dissect a few scenes.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.640
Nate, what do you want
to talk about today?

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.110
Nate Lee: So, I brought in

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.160
the episode 11 from season
four, "Crawl Space."

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.130
It's one of my favorite episodes

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.220
just because so many things happened,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.970
and also technically,

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:42.770
I think it's definitely
something worth talking about.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.730
It is very, very interesting.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.290
And, before we talk about it,
I think we need to sort of

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:48.840
talk about the cinematographer.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.340
I think he's played such an important role

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.580
in the show, right?

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.220
So, how I wanna talk about
this is, according to him,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.540
there were three big influences

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.695
when he was filming "Breaking Bad."

00:01:01.695 --> 00:01:04.130
One is, surprisingly, "Godfather."

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:06.780
It's very different visually,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.970
but actually there's a lot in common.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.220
And I think it's especially evident

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.740
in a lot of interior scenes.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.200
So, we're gonna look at this
scene from "Crawl Space."

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.960
It's when Walter is talking to Tyrus,

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.331
but I mostly wanna talk
about lighting here.

00:01:23.331 --> 00:01:26.300
So, what "Godfather" is very famous for

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.155
is it was the very first film that did

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.287
a very unique sort of lighting,

00:01:31.287 --> 00:01:34.987
which was sort of the very
minimal overhead lighting,

00:01:34.987 --> 00:01:36.178
and if you actually see,

00:01:36.178 --> 00:01:39.410
especially in a lot of the meth lab scene,

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.610
is that they actually use
a lot of overhead lights.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.062
If you actually see the
overall lighting in the place,

00:01:45.062 --> 00:01:47.350
there's a lot of lamps right above.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.378
You can see a lot of that,

00:01:49.378 --> 00:01:52.303
and especially if you look at right here,

00:01:52.303 --> 00:01:54.761
you can see shadows,
a lot of shadows here.

00:01:54.761 --> 00:01:58.560
A lot of shadows, which
is very kind of peculiar,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.500
'cause a lot of TV shows and movies,

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.000
they light the face a lot.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.830
And also that's why they use reflectors

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.250
to sort of bounce the light off.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:07.915
But actually what's funny is instead of

00:02:07.915 --> 00:02:10.750
bouncing the light onto the
face, they bounce it off,

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:12.307
and then it sort of gets dispersed here.

00:02:12.307 --> 00:02:15.390
And a lot of lighting
is just on their body

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.910
and not their face, and what it does is

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.880
it adds sort of a complexity

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.615
to the lighting.

00:02:23.615 --> 00:02:25.430
You know, and I think a lot of problem

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.580
that I have with modern television

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.695
is everything is so well lit.

00:02:29.695 --> 00:02:33.603
"Breaking Bad" uses a lot of
different shadows and lights,

00:02:33.603 --> 00:02:36.380
and there's definitely a
contrast to it, and also,

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.880
if you look at it a lot,
you can definitely see--

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.847
Carter: Yeah, like right there? So dark.

00:02:41.847 --> 00:02:45.338
Nate: Yeah. Especially in the
close-up scenes of the faces,

00:02:45.338 --> 00:02:48.180
you can definitely see
sort of a line forming.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.070
The light is obviously coming from here.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.263
There's no direct light
coming onto his face.

00:02:53.263 --> 00:02:54.096
Carter: Yeah, you see the outline.

00:02:54.096 --> 00:02:57.495
Nate: Right, right, so it sort
of divides his face in half,

00:02:57.495 --> 00:02:59.597
where one part of his
face is sort of lit up

00:02:59.597 --> 00:03:02.710
but another part of his face is not.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.210
And that's actually also
something that "Godfather"

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:06.905
sort of uses.

00:03:06.905 --> 00:03:07.815
Carter: OK, yeah.

00:03:07.815 --> 00:03:09.250
Nate: Albeit a little differently.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.780
So, "Godfather" cinematography
by Gordon Willis,

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.570
who is a very important figure

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.270
in the history of cinematography,

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:20.460
also uses a lot of shadows,

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.338
and he was, in a lot of ways, one of the

00:03:23.338 --> 00:03:27.210
very first person to do this
in mainstream, big cinema.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:31.480
And how he used it a lot is,
again, he used overhead shots.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.560
And that, what it does is
it puts a lot of shadowing

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:35.500
near the eyes.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:38.290
Actually, there's a very
funny story where someone

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.320
from the studio said, "You
know, I can't see the eye.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.090
Like, I can't see what's happening."

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:45.210
And the director and the
cinematographer would say

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.490
that it doesn't really matter.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.060
He really wanted to focus sort of on

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.900
the entire expression of the
face rather than the eye,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.675
which is usually how
actors these days act,

00:03:55.675 --> 00:03:56.725
using their eyes.

00:03:56.725 --> 00:03:58.740
But he actually really wanted to focus

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.338
on using the entire face to sort of act.

00:04:01.338 --> 00:04:02.650
Carter: So, the lighting
that you're talking about

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:05.070
really accentuates
certain parts of his face

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:06.710
and gives it more depth.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:10.150
You see the shadows
causing the deepest blacks

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:11.070
and really like an abyss

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:14.080
and really conveys his emotion as pain.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.390
Nate: Right. It really
makes it more complex

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:19.410
'cause you have, like, places
where it's really well lit

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:20.620
and then where it's half lit

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.741
and then where it's sort
of like very darkly lit.

00:04:22.741 --> 00:04:25.191
So it's like, it does add complexity

00:04:25.191 --> 00:04:26.441
and, as you said, depth.

00:04:26.441 --> 00:04:27.410
So that was it for me.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:28.670
What did you want to talk about, Carter?

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:29.940
Carter: I want to talk about a scene

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.862
from season three, episode one, "No Más."

00:04:32.862 --> 00:04:33.762
[truck breaks squealing]

00:04:35.746 --> 00:04:36.587
[truck door slamming]

00:04:37.744 --> 00:04:39.080
Man: What the hell's going on in there?

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.440
Carter: So, in this scene,
the Salamanca cousins

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.402
are arriving from Mexico,

00:04:44.402 --> 00:04:46.825
going across the border,
getting smuggled in

00:04:46.825 --> 00:04:49.300
in this truck full of hay
with some other immigrants.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.040
I wanna talk about this scene right here.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:56.410
We get a great frame within a frame,

00:04:56.410 --> 00:05:00.390
so what I mean by that is
the camera's inside the truck

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.820
and you get this natural frame
of the back of the hay truck

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:08.820
that points your eye
towards the action here.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.540
Nate: Now that I'm just
looking at the frame,

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.530
do you know that ending
scene in "Searchers"?

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:15.480
It's another, like, very famous Western.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:17.210
Yeah, which has been very influential,

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.590
and I'm seeing this resembles that a lot.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:23.690
Carter: Yeah, so then as we continue here,

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:26.750
we go to a very interesting shot.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:30.319
We get to this giant, wide shot.

00:05:30.319 --> 00:05:34.040
Our eyes are looking at
just these small, small

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:35.970
figures in this vast landscape.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.600
So, why do you think they
chose to go to this shot?

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.208
Nate: We're, in a way, sort of

00:05:41.208 --> 00:05:42.649
turning away from the violence.

00:05:42.649 --> 00:05:44.430
We're taking a step back and looking at it

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:48.530
and sort of showing how this
violence is so meaningless,

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:51.680
how it's so quick, and when
you look at it from far away,

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.590
it's just nothing.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:55.450
When, in fact, if we had a close-up,

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:58.880
it would have had a huge impact, you know,

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.540
seeing this, like, intense
moment, up-close scene, the fear.

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:04.830
But there's almost no
emotion in this scene.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:05.663
That's what I'm getting.

00:06:05.663 --> 00:06:06.960
Carter: I definitely agree with that.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:10.640
It's clear that it's an
average day for them, almost.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.440
They've done this many times.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:14.580
Nate: That makes the
violence even more brutal.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.000
'Cause, like, you know, in
horror movies it's always about

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.450
what you imagine is worse
than what you actually see.

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:22.620
Carter: The cinematographer
actually singled this shot out

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.710
in a couple of interviews
as one of his favorites,

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.155
and what he said was:

00:06:43.104 --> 00:06:44.440
[gun bangs]

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:51.340
So, I thought that was another
really interesting point

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:55.130
that he raised up, that the
audience is trained by media

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.185
and probably by "Breaking Bad" as well,

00:06:57.185 --> 00:06:59.510
that they're gonna get this violence

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:02.300
and get, you know, a close-up
of someone getting shot.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.890
Nate: They're sort of playing
with our expectations.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:05.646
Carter: They're playing
with our expectations,

00:07:05.646 --> 00:07:10.067
and also just a great shot
of the composition of,

00:07:10.067 --> 00:07:12.200
you know, just a built-in dichotomy

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.608
between the beauty of the scene

00:07:13.608 --> 00:07:16.882
and the violence and death
that the cousins are bringing.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:21.250
Nate: So, during the Emmys, the new teaser

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:22.680
for "El Camino" dropped.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.970
It didn't seem like there was a lot,

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:27.130
but it seemed like the
fans were picking it apart.

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:29.320
And they actually found
a surprising number

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:31.564
of sort of hints and clues.

00:07:31.564 --> 00:07:32.690
Did you notice anything?

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:34.120
Carter: Yeah, I did notice something.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.720
So, let's take a look here.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.860
So, first of all, this is the El Camino

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.480
that Jesse escaped in

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:46.000
after the last episode of "Breaking Bad."

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.800
That's Todd's El Camino.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.700
So, right here, someone on Reddit actually

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:51.091
lightened the image

00:07:51.091 --> 00:07:55.507
and found some of the same rock formations

00:07:55.507 --> 00:07:56.960
in the background

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.440
and found out that it was
actually the exact same place

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.900
where Jesse and Walt first cooked,

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:04.108
which is also where
"Ozymandias" took place,

00:08:04.108 --> 00:08:05.274
where Hank died,

00:08:05.274 --> 00:08:07.210
and where Walt buried his money,

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:10.320
and then on the radio they're
talking about nine bodies.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.560
So, we took a look back
at the finale to confirm,

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.170
and it is eight Nazis that Walt killed,

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:18.380
and then Walt himself.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:20.150
So that would be nine.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:21.720
So that's it for this teaser,

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.400
but a full trailer actually
came out the next day,

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.000
and our team here at
Insider broke that down.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.530
So if you wanna watch that,
check out our YouTube channel.

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:30.450
Nate: That's it for today.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:32.450
We'll see you next time.

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:35.550
You know, studios should
really let the filmmakers

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:37.810
do their job and not get in the way.

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:39.280
Carter: Yeah, studios,
if you're listening.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:40.113
Nate: Studios.

